    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "After the democratic South African government came into power in 1994, the education system\r\nfaced the challenge to redress socioeconomic disadvantages inherited from apartheid. My\r\nresearch examines one component of the new education system: the distribution of educators. I\r\nuse government data not publicly available to document educator distribution trends among\r\nschools of different levels of poverty in two provinces, Gauteng and the Western Cape. The\r\nquantity and level of qualifications of educators are measured, and the trends are linked to\r\nstudent outcomes. The results are consistent across both provinces: Descriptive statistics and\r\nregression analysis finds that, on average, schools with relatively less poverty experience a lower\r\npupil-educator ratio and have educators of higher qualifications. A contributor to this inequality\r\nis the practice of local school governing bodies in relatively wealthier schools hiring additional\r\neducators beyond what the state provides. State provision is itself a contributor to inequality in\r\nsome cases. The impact of the quantity of educators and of the level of qualifications on student\r\noutcomes is unclear. Ultimately, my research suggests that the current distribution of educators\r\n\r\nin South Africa falls short of the principles of equality and redress at the foundation of the post-\r\napartheid education system.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Pon, A. (2022). Equality and Redress in Education: Educator Allocation in Post-Apartheid South Africa. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/wb290qf0216",
            "type": "preferred citation",
